# Line endings.
#
# `git push` on Windows warns "LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git
# touches it". That warning is harmless for the JS and SQL in this repo, but it
# is not harmless for supabase/run_tests.sh: a shell script committed with CRLF
# fails on Linux with `bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M`, and CI is Linux. The
# failure reads as a missing interpreter rather than as a line-ending problem,
# which is what makes it worth pinning rather than remembering.
#
# `text=auto` lets Git normalise to LF in the repository while leaving the
# working copy to whatever the platform prefers. The `.sh` rule is stricter: LF
# everywhere, including in the checkout, because that file is executed by bash
# no matter which machine it was checked out on.

* text=auto
*.sh text eol=lf

# Binary, so Git never tries to normalise them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
